WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.469
Several hours had passed since the train had

2
00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:08.921
left Fix and Aouda at Fort Kearney station. The weather was

3
00:00:08.922 --> 00:00:13.459
dismal. Fix sat motionless on a bench in the

4
00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:19.757
station. Aouda, despite the bitter cold, kept stepping outside, walking to the end of

5
00:00:19.758 --> 00:00:28.775
the platform and peering through the heavily falling snow. She would return chilled and remain inside only long enough to warm up.

6
00:00:28.776 --> 00:00:32.064
Then she would exit again, looking for any sign

7
00:00:32.065 --> 00:00:39.656
of the rescue party. The commander of the fort waited with them, careful to hide his worry. Night fell, but

8
00:00:39.657 --> 00:00:44.735
the little band of rescuers still did not return. As the falling

9
00:00:44.736 --> 00:00:46.605
snow diminished, the

10
00:00:46.606 --> 00:00:51.096
air became intensely cold. Where could they be? Had they found

11
00:00:51.097 --> 00:00:57.459
the Indians? Were they fighting with them, or were they still wandering in the cold? Aouda

12
00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:05.154
suffered through the night. Her heart was filled with anguish, and her imagination was full of the dangers

13
00:01:05.155 --> 00:01:07.748
Mr. Fogg faced. At dawn the sun

14
00:01:07.749 --> 00:01:17.223
rose above a misty horizon. It was now possible to see objects two miles off, but the horizon was

15
00:01:17.224 --> 00:01:22.468
still vacant. At seven o'clock the captain betrayed his worry and began to rub

16
00:01:22.469 --> 00:01:25.935
his hands anxiously. Then gunshots were heard. Was it

17
00:01:25.936 --> 00:01:32.224
a signal? The soldiers who had remained in the fort rushed out. Half a mile to the south, they could see a

18
00:01:32.225 --> 00:01:36.451
small group approaching. Mr. Fogg was riding at the

19
00:01:36.452 --> 00:01:44.589
head of the squad, and just behind him were Passepartout and the other two missing passengers. They had met and

20
00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:47.818
fought the Indians ten miles south of Fort Kearney.

21
00:01:47.819 --> 00:01:53.130
Passepartout and his companions had managed to escape their ropes and were already

22
00:01:53.131 --> 00:01:58.307
struggling against the Indians when Mr. Fogg and the soldiers arrived. The

23
00:01:58.308 --> 00:02:03.080
fight had been quick and successful. They were all welcomed

24
00:02:03.081 --> 00:02:07.317
back at the station with a joyful cheer. Phileas Fogg distributed

25
00:02:07.318 --> 00:02:13.233
the reward he had promised to the soldiers. Aouda took

26
00:02:13.234 --> 00:02:16.342
her protector's hand and pressed it to her

27
00:02:16.343 --> 00:02:19.327
own, too moved even

28
00:02:19.328 --> 00:02:24.085
to speak. Meanwhile Passepartout was looking around for the train. He

29
00:02:24.086 --> 00:02:29.266
thought he would find it there, ready to start for Omaha.

30
00:02:29.267 --> 00:02:32.118
He hoped that they could make up the lost

31
00:02:32.119 --> 00:02:35.849
time. "The train! The train!" cried Passepartout. "Gone,"

32
00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:38.265
said Fix. "And when

33
00:02:38.266 --> 00:02:41.996
does the next train pass here?" asked

34
00:02:41.997 --> 00:02:45.709
Mr. Fogg. "Not until this evening," explained

35
00:02:45.710 --> 00:02:50.460
Aouda. "Ah," said Mr. Fogg quietly. They were

36
00:02:50.461 --> 00:02:56.223
now twenty hours behind schedule. Fix approached Mr. Fogg with a possible solution. While Fix

37
00:02:56.224 --> 00:02:59.687
had been sitting in the station, a

38
00:02:59.688 --> 00:03:03.325
man had approached him and offered him a ride on a sledge

39
00:03:03.326 --> 00:03:11.210
with sails. Fix pointed to the man, who was walking up and down in front

40
00:03:11.211 --> 00:03:17.052
of the station. Mr. Fogg paused. He had never heard of any vehicle like the one described.

41
00:03:17.053 --> 00:03:21.281
He went up to the man, and after a brief

42
00:03:21.282 --> 00:03:26.027
exchange, the two entered a hut a short distance from the fort. Mr.

43
00:03:26.028 --> 00:03:30.605
Fogg examined the curious device. A low frame was mounted on

44
00:03:30.606 --> 00:03:37.924
two long runners, like skis. A high mast was attached to the frame, held firmly by thin metal wires. Attached to

45
00:03:37.925 --> 00:03:42.067
the mast was a wide sail. The owner

46
00:03:42.068 --> 00:03:45.880
of the vehicle, a man named Mudge, said

47
00:03:45.881 --> 00:03:51.332
the sledge could take them to Omaha where they could catch a train to Chicago and

48
00:03:51.333 --> 00:03:55.934
finally to New York. Mr. Fogg made a bargain with Mudge. At eight o'clock the sledge

49
00:03:55.935 --> 00:04:04.540
was ready. Mr. Fogg, Aouda, Passepartout, and Fix, took their places. The great sail was hoisted, and soon they were

50
00:04:04.541 --> 00:04:07.317
skimming across the frozen prairie at forty miles

51
00:04:07.318 --> 00:04:11.470
an hour. The travelers huddled close together to protect themselves from

52
00:04:11.471 --> 00:04:16.423
the cold. The freezing wind burned Passepartout's face as red as the setting sun.

53
00:04:16.424 --> 00:04:22.707
Aouda was cozily wrapped in fur. Mr. Fogg was as calm and confident as usual, sitting

54
00:04:22.708 --> 00:04:28.702
close beside Aouda. Fix was happy to be moving closer to England with Mr.

55
00:04:28.703 --> 00:04:32.266
Fogg in his sight. Passepartout knew that he would never

56
00:04:32.267 --> 00:04:34.812
forget Mr. Fogg's

57
00:04:34.813 --> 00:04:38.194
bravery. Nor would he forget the sacrifice that Mr. Fogg

58
00:04:38.195 --> 00:04:42.235
had made to rescue him from the Indians. Without hesitation,

59
00:04:42.236 --> 00:04:46.209
Fogg had risked his fortune and his life! Mudge

60
00:04:46.210 --> 00:04:53.335
steered his strange vehicle skillfully. Traveling on skis, they were able to cross the frozen Platte River and shorten

61
00:04:53.336 --> 00:05:00.223
the distance they would have traveled by train. They arrived at the station in Omaha

62
00:05:00.224 --> 00:05:03.242
just in time to catch the next train

63
00:05:03.243 --> 00:05:05.972
to Chicago. Mr. Fogg

64
00:05:05.973 --> 00:05:09.807
generously rewarded Mudge, and they jumped aboard the train

65
00:05:09.808 --> 00:05:12.902
as it slowly pulled out of the

66
00:05:12.903 --> 00:05:20.280
station. Passepartout's Guidebook What a strange way to travel! I've seen

67
00:05:20.281 --> 00:05:25.436
a sledge before, but I've never seen a sledge with sails! A sledge is a vehicle that

68
00:05:25.437 --> 00:05:31.272
slides along grass or snow. It does not have wheels, but instead has runners, which are like

69
00:05:31.273 --> 00:05:36.069
skis. A sledge can carry heavy things such as wood, stone, or blocks of

70
00:05:36.070 --> 00:05:41.913
ice. Many American farmers have sledges, which can be moved by people, animals, or even sails on a

71
00:05:41.914 --> 00:05:50.595
windy day! A sledge is similar to a sled or a sleigh. Have you ridden a sled in winter? It's fun, isn't

72
00:05:50.596 --> 00:05:56.361
it? A sled is smaller than a sledge or a sleigh and is usually used for fun or

73
00:05:56.362 --> 00:06:00.414
to carry a light load. Animals, like horses or dogs,

74
00:06:00.415 --> 00:06:04.052
or people can pull sleds. A sleigh is much larger and is usually pulled

75
00:06:04.053 --> 00:06:13.194
by horses. What a fabulous adventure! I never expected to find myself sailing—and then skiing—across the American prairies!

